# {{sid|en|Q283}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} An inorganic compound (of molecular formula {{chemf|H2O}}) found at [[room temperature]] and pressure as a clear [[liquid]]; it is present naturally as [[rain#Noun|rain]], and found in [[river]]s, [[lake]]s and [[sea]]s; its solid form is [[ice#Noun|ice]] and its gaseous form is [[steam#Noun|steam]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:water}}
#: {{hypo|en|ice|steam|water vapor//water vapour|;|light water|semi-heavy water|heavy water|super-heavy water}}
#: {{mero|en|hydrogen|oxygen}}
#: {{ux|en|By the action of electricity, the '''water''' was resolved into its two parts, oxygen and hydrogen.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1805|month=December|author=Julius Griffiths|title=A Journey across the Desert|journal=The Monthly Mirror|page=362
|passage=It is wholly out of the power of language to convey any idea of the blissful enjoyment of obtaining '''water''', after an almost total want of it, during eight and forty hours, in the scorching regions of an Arabian desert, in the month of July.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013|month=September-October|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/katie-l-burke Katie L. Burke]| magazine=w:American Scientist| url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/5/in-the-news-47|title=In the News| passage=Oxygen levels on Earth skyrocketed 2.4 billion years ago, when cyanobacteria evolved photosynthesis: the ability to convert '''water''' and carbon dioxide into carbohydrates and waste oxygen using solar energy.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130903041000/https://www.americanscientist.org/issues/pub/2013/5/in-the-news-47}}
