===Noun===
{{en-noun}}
[[File:Map of First French Empire and Vassals States.png|thumb|A map showing The First French Empire in 1810.]]

# A [[political]] [[state]], often a [[monarchy]], that has [[achieve]]d a much greater [[current]] [[size]] than its [[initial]] size by [[conquer]]ing [[surround]]ing [[territory|territories]], [[city|cities]] or [[nation]]s.
#: {{ux|en|the Russian '''empire'''}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=February 7, 2022|author=Charles Hugh Smith|work=How Empires Die|url=https://charleshughsmith.blogspot.com/2022/02/how-empires-die.html|text=States and '''empires''' fail when they are no longer the solution, they are the problem.}}
# A political [[unit]] [[rule]]d by an [[emperor]] or [[empress]].
#: {{ux|en|The '''Empire''' of Vietnam was a short-lived client state of Japan governing Vietnam between March 11 and August 23, 1945.}}
# The [[group]] of states or other territories that [[owe]] [[allegiance]] to an [[imperial]] [[power]] ([[foreign]] to them), when [[distinguish]]ed from the [[native]] territory of that power; imperial [[possession]]s.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2001-08-02|author=P. J. Marshall|title=The Cambridge Illustrated History of the British Empire|publisher=Cambridge University Press|isbn=9780521002547|page=105
|text=British people [...] continued to believe in empire. It was what gave Britain a unique role in the world, and in return Britain had drawn strength from its '''empire''' to enable it to survive two great wars that had wrecked so many of its competitors. Imperial management in the twentieth{{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=2021-04-13|author=Daniel Verdier|title=Democracy and International Trade: Britain, France, and the United States, 1860-1990|publisher=Princeton University Press|isbn=9780691228181|page=139
|text=Since Britain imported mainly foodstuffs from its '''empire''', no preferences could be granted to the colonies without Britain first imposing a tariff on foodstuffs imported from other countries.}}
# An [[expansive]] and [[powerful]] [[enterprise]] under the [[control]] of one [[person]] or group.
#: {{ux|en|the McDonald's fast food '''empire'''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002|author=Evelyn L. Damore|title=The Rattle and Hiss of the Tin Gods|publisher=iUniverse|isbn=9780595228447|page=111
|passage=“Revenues for Jackson's non-profit '''empire''' sky-rocketed from $4 million in 1997, to more than $14 million just two years later.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Martin Short|title=The Rise of the Mafia|publisher=Kings Road Publishing|isbn=9781843582236
|passage=The Mafia never forgave Castro but Lansky had already laid the foundations of a mob gambling '''empire''' all over the Caribbean {{...}}}}
# ([[absolute|Absolute]]) control, [[dominion]], [[sway]].
#* {{RQ:Walpole Mysterious Mother|1|1|passage=Each master whim maintains its hour of '''empire''',<br>And obstinately faithful to its dictates}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1881|author=François Guizot|title=The History of Civilization from the Fall of the Roman Empire to the French Revolution...|page=122
|passage=The brutality, the unthinking, the unreflecting character of the barbarians were so great, that the new faith, the new feelings with which they had been inspired, exercised but a very slight '''empire''' over them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:H.G. Wells|title=w:The War of the Worlds|publisher=William Heinemann|location=London|page=1|text=With infinite complacency men went to and fro over this globe about their little affairs, serene in their assurance of their '''empire''' over matter.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Stefania Tutino|title=Empire of Souls: Robert Bellarmine and the Christian Commonwealth|publisher=Oxford University Press|isbn=9780199780587|page=270
|passage={{...}} could gain some political strength for the pope, but in so doing the pope would lose the uniqueness and supremacy of his '''empire''' over souls: {{...}}}}

