{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To move [[downwards]], to [[fall]], to [[drop]].
#: {{ux|en|The dollar has '''declined''' rapidly since 2001.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1978|last=Kołakowski|first=Leszek|authorlink=Leszek Kołakowski|title=w:Main Currents of Marxism|volume=I: The Founders|origlang=pl|original=Główne nurty marksizmu: powstanie|translator=P[aul] S[teven] Falla|origyear=1976|publisher=w:Clarendon Press|location=w:Oxford|isbn=9780198245476|oclc=181789472|page=298|passage=Only empirical observation, and not deduction from the nature of the profit rate, can tell us whether it does tend permanently to '''decline'''; and such observation is not found to confirm Marx's theory.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[weak]]er or [[worse]].
#: {{ux|en|My health '''declined''' in winter.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=13 Feb 82|volume=9|number=29|journal=Gay Community News|page=10|title=Connie|author=Nancy Walker|text=After her sother<!--mother?--> died, Connie began to '''decline''' noticeably.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2026 February 18|author=Paul Clifton|title=West of England Line problems laid bare|journal=RAIL|page=7|text=Will this decision-making paralysis continue until Great British Railways has been established and settled into a new rhythm? If so, passengers on the West of England line are in for a rough ride, as the reliability of these tired old British Rail trains inevitably '''declines''', and maintenance costs rise.}} 
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To bend downward; to bring down; to depress; to cause to bend, or fall.
#* {{RQ:Thomson Spring|year=1768|passage=in melancholy site, with head '''declined'''}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=IX|stanza=10|passage=And now faire Phoebus gan '''decline''' in hast / His weary wagon to the Westerne vale.}}
