====Translations====
{{trans-top|upright}}
* Spanish: {{t+|es|redondo}}, {{t|es|redondillo}}, {{t|es|romanillo}}
{{trans-bottom}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|typography}} One of the main three [[type]]s used for the [[Latin alphabet]] (the others being [[italics]] and [[blackletter]]), in which the [[ascender]]s are mostly straight.
# {{lb|en|chiefly|American typesetting|;|uncommon outside that}} {{ellipsis of|en|roman numeral}}.
#: {{cot|en|arabic#Noun}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|countable}} A [[novel]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=21 Dec 86|volume=14|number=23|journal=Gay Community News|page=7|author=Michael Bronski|title=Buddy Stories Your Brother Never Told You|text=What raises ''One Last Waltz'' far above the usual family '''''roman''''' is not just the gimmick of the ancient fable in modern clothes {{...}} but Mordden's language and his sheer joy at telling a story.}}
#* '''2014''', "[https://books.google.com/books?id=FPdRAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA942&lpg=PA942&dq=roman+meaning+novel&source=bl&ots=ZevlCANlxe&sig=ACfU3U2VsKpPjy-fqn32eD-IY3Vv9uCcQw&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwj78Lf8_cPqAhU5HjQIHZYHCiA4ChDoATAFegQIChAB#v=onepage&q=roman%20meaning%20novel&f=false Novel and Romance: Etymologies]". Heyworth, Gregory; Logan, Peter Melville (ed.). ''[https://www.amazon.com/Encyclopedia-Novel-Wiley-blackwell-Literature/dp/1118723899 Encyclopedia of the Novel]'', Hoboken, NJ: Wiley-Blackwell, p. 942. {{ISBN|978-1118723890}} <!-- Data on Google Books is wrong. The Routledge version has one edition, it seems, and fewer pages than 942. -->
#*: Samuel Johnson, writing in his ''Dictionary of the English Language'' (1755), [defined] "novel [as] a small tale, generally of love." To modern sensibilities, Johnson's novel resembles more closely the novella in dimension and the romance in substance. [...] [T]he term ''romance'', or '''''roman''''', once interchangeable with ''novel'' in English, retains the meaning of ''novel'' in Germany, France, Russia, and most of Europe, while in the anglophone world it has been demoted to frivolity.

