{{inh+|fr|fro|romanz|t=common language (as opposed to Latin)}}, from {{inh|fr|la-vul|romanicē|t=in the way of the [[Romans]] (as opposed to the [[Franks]])}}, from {{der|fr|la|rōmānicus}}, from {{der|fr|la|rōmānus}}.<ref>{{cite-book|fr|last=Dauzat|first=Albert|coauthors=Jean Dubois, Henri Mitterand|title=Nouveau dictionnaire étymologique|year=1964|publisher=Librairie Larousse|location=Paris|chapter=''roman''}}</ref>
The meaning “common language” changed into “book in common language” and then into “adventure novel”.<ref name="picoche"/> See also {{doublet|fr|romance|notext=1}}, of the same ultimate origin but borrowed through Spanish.

