{{also|Lord|LORD}}
==English==
{{wp}}
{{Wikiquote|Lord}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:lord|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|lord}} and {{m|enm|lorde}} (attested from the 15th century), from earlier (14th century) {{m|enm|lourde}} and other variants which dropped the intervocalic consonant of earlier {{m|enm|lowerd}}, {{m|enm|louerd}}, {{m|enm|loverd}}, {{m|enm|laford}}, and {{m|enm|lhoaverd}}; from {{inh|en|ang|hlāford}} < {{m|ang|hlāfweard}}, a compound of {{m|ang|hlāf||bread}} + {{m|ang|weard||guardian}}; see {{m|en|loaf}} and {{m|en|ward}}. The term was already being applied broadly prior to the literary development of Old English and was influenced by its common use to translate Latin {{m|la|dominus}}. Compare {{cog|sco|laird||lord}}, preserving a separate vowel development (from northern/Scottish {{cog|enm|lard}}, {{m|enm|laverd}}), the Old English compound {{m|ang|hlāfǣta|hlāf-ǣta|[[servant]]|lit=[[bread]]-[[eater]]}}, and modern English {{m|en|lady}}, from Old English {{m|ang|hlǣfdīġe||[[bread]]-[[kneader]]}}.<ref>''Oxford English Dictionary'', 1st&nbsp;ed. "lord, ''n.''". Oxford University Press (Oxford), 1903.</ref> The Middle English word ''laford'' was borrowed by Icelandic, where it survives as {{m|is|lávarður}}. {{doublet|en|hlaford|laird}}.

===Pronunciation===
