* {{homophones|en|laud|aa=non-rhotic}}
* {{homophones|en|lured|aa=some accents}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)d|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[master]] of the [[servant]]s of a [[household]]; {{lb|en|historical}} the [[master]] of a [[feudal]] [[manor]]
#* {{RQ:KJV|Matthew|24|46|text=[[blessed|Bleſſed]] is that [[servant|ſeruant]], [[whom]]e his '''Lord''' when he [[comes|commeth]], [[shall|ſhal]] [[find]]e [[so|ſo]] doing.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|III|2|line=167|text=''Por''. ...But now, I was the '''Lord'''<br>of this [[fair]]e [[mansion|manſion]], [[master|maiſter]] of my [[servants|ſeruants]],<br>[[queen|Queene]] [[over|oer]] [[myself|my ſelfe]]{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|II|418|text='''Lords''' of manors are distinguished from other land-owners with regard to the [[game]].}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} The [[male]] [[head]] of a [[household]], a [[father]] or [[husband]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|text={{...}}thou [[worthy|worthie]] '''Lord''',<br>Of that [[unworthy|vnworthie]] wife that [[greets|greeteth]] thee}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|V|2|line=131|text=''Pet. Katherine'', I charge thee, tell [[these|theſe]] [[head-strong|head-ſtrong]] women,<br>What [[duty|dutie]] they [[do]]e owe their '''Lords''' and [[husbands|huſbands]]!}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|18|12|text=Therefore Sarah laughed within her [[self|ſelfe]], [[saying|ſaying]], After I am [[wax]]ed old, [[shall|ſhall]] I [[have|haue]] [[pleasure|pleaſure]], my '''lord''' being old [[also|alſo]]?}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Emma|III|xvi|page=300|text=Yes, here I am, my good friend; and here I have been so long, that anywhere else I should think it necessary to [[apologise]]; but, the truth is, that I am waiting for my '''lord''' and master.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} The [[owner]] of a [[house]], [[piece]] of [[land]], or [[other]] [[possession]]
##* '''''ante''&nbsp;1637''', {{w|Ben Jonson}}, ''Sad Shepherd'', ii. i. 36
##*: A [[mighty|mightie]] '''Lord''' of Swine!
##* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|12|text=Turnus{{...}}<br>Wrench'd from his feeble hold the shining Sword;<br>And plung'd it in the Bosom of its '''Lord'''.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1874|author=J. H. Collins|title=Principles of Metal Mining|year_published=1875|journal=Gloss|section=139/2
|passage='''''Lord''''', the owner of the land in which a mine is situated is called the ‘'''lord'''’.}}
# One [[possess]]ing [[similar]] [[mastery]] over [[others]]; {{lb|en|historical}} any [[feudal]] [[superior]] [[generally]]; any [[nobleman]] or [[aristocrat]]; any [[chief]], [[prince]], or [[sovereign]] [[ruler]]; in Scotland, a male member of the lowest [[rank]] of [[nobility]] (the equivalent rank in England is ''[[baron]]'')
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1530|author=John Palsgrave|title=Lesclarcissement|section=680/1
|passage=It is a [[piteous|pytuouse]] case... [[when|whan]] [[subjects|subjectes]] [[rebel]]l [[against|agaynst]] their [[natural]]l '''lorde'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=xii|passage=70|text=Man over men He made not '''Lord'''.|year=1873}}
## {{lb|en|historical}} A [[feudal]] [[tenant]] [[holding]] his [[manor]] [[directly]] of the [[king]]
## A [[peer of the realm]], [[particularly]] a [[temporal]] one
##* '''''ante'' 1420''', T. Hoccleve, ''De Regimine Principum'', 442
##*: Men [[might|myghten]] '''lordis''' [[know]]e
##*: By [[their|there]] [[array]]e, from [[other|oþir]] [[folk]]e.
##* '''1453''', Rolls of Parliament, V. 266/2
##*: If such [[person]]e [[be]]e of the estate of a '''Lord''', as [[duke|Duc]], [[marquess|Marques]], [[earl|Erle]], [[viscount|Viscount]] or [[baron|Baron]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|IV|1|line=18|text=Princes, and noble '''Lords''':<br>What [[answer|anſwer]] [[shall|ſhall]] I make to this [[base|baſe]] man?}}
##* {{RQ:Selden Titles of Honor|chapter=III|page=59|passage=In origination of our Engliſh name '''''Lord''''', vvhereby vve and the Scots [[style#Verb|ſtile]] all ſuch as are of the Greater Nobilitie i. ''Barons'', as alſo Biſhops, its not eaſie to ſatisfie you.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1900 July 21|journal=Daily Express|text=The Englishman of to-day still dearly loves a '''lord'''.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|uncommon}} A [[baron]] or [[lesser]] [[nobleman]], [[as opposed to]] [[greater]] ones
##* '''1526''', W. Bonde, ''Pylgrimage of Perfection'', i. sig. Bviiiv
##*: [[far|Farre]] [[excelling|excellyng]] the state of '''lordes''', [[earls|erles]], dukes or [[kings|kynges]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1826|author=w:Benjamin Disraeli|title=Vivian Grey|section=II. iii. iii. 26
|passage=The Marquess played off the two '''Lords''' and the Baronet against his former friend.}}
# One [[possess]]ing [[similar]] [[mastery]] in [[figurative]] [[sense]]s (''esp. as'' '''lord of''' ~)
#* '''''ante''&nbsp;1300''', ''Cursor Mundi'', 782
#*: O wityng [[both|bath]] [[good|god]] and ill Ȝee suld be '''lauerds''' at ȝour will.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1398|translator=John Trevisa|author=Bartholomew de Glanville|title=De Proprietatibus Rerum|year_published=1495|section=viii. xvi. 322
|passage=The [[sun|sonne]] is the '''lorde''' of [[planets|planetes]].}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|3|text=Love is '''Lord''' of all.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Kenneth Graham|title=w:The Golden Age|location=London|year=1895|page=8|passage=The masterful wind was up and out, shouting and chasing, the '''lord''' of the morning.}}
#* '''1992''' November 18, {{w|Larry David}}, ''{{w|Seinfeld}}'', 4.11: "{{w|The Contest}}":
#*: But are you still master of your domain?
#*: I am king of the county. You?
#*: '''Lord''' of the manor.
## A [[magnate]] of a [[trade]] or [[profession]].
##: ''The {{w|Tobacco Lords}} were a group of Scottish merchants and slave traders who in the 18th century made enormous fortunes by trading in tobacco.''
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1823|author=W. Cobbett|title=Rural Rides|year_published=1885|section=I. 399
|passage=Oh, Oh! The cotton '''Lords''' are tearing!}}
# {{lb|en|astrology}} The [[heavenly body]] [[consider]]ed to [[possess]] a [[dominant]] [[influence]] over an [[event]], [[time]], [[etc.]]
#* {{quote-text|en|year=c. 1391|author=w:Geoffrey Chaucer|title=Treatise on the Astrolabe|section=ii. §4
|passage=The [[ascendant|assendent]], & eke the '''lord''' of the [[ascendant|assendent]], may be shapen for to be [[fortunate|fortunat]] or [[unfortunate|infortunat]], as thus, a [[fortunate|fortunat]] [[ascendant|assendent]] clepen they [[when|whan]] [[that|þat]] no [[wicked|wykkid]] [[planet]]e, as [[Saturn]]e or Mars, or [[else|elles]] the tail of the [[dragon|dragoun]], is in [[the|þe]] [[house|hows]] of the [[ascendant|assendent]].}}
# {{lb|en|British|slang|obsolete}} A [[hunchback]].
#* '''1699''', B.E., ''A new dictionary of the terms ancient and modern of the canting crew'':
#*: '''''Lord''''', a very crooked, deformed... Person.
# {{lb|en|British|Australian|via [[Cockney rhyming slang]]|obsolete}} [[sixpence|Sixpence]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=November 16 1933|journal=Times Literary Supplement|section=782/1
|passage=Twenty years ago you might hear a sixpence described as a ‘'''Lord'''’ meaning ‘[[lord of the manor|Lord of the Manor]]’; that is, a [[tanner]].}}

