# The [[sky]], ''[[specifically]]'':
## {{lb|en|dated|_|or|_|poetic|now|usually|in the plural}} The [[distant]] [[sky]] in which the [[sun]], [[moon]], and [[star]]s [[appear]] or [[move]]; the [[firmament]]; the [[celestial sphere]]s.
##* '''1535''', {{w|Coverdale Bible}}, {{w|Ecclesiastes}} 3:1:
##*: All that is [[under|vnder]] '''the heauen'''.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1585|translator=Thomas Washington|author=Nicholas de Nicolay|title=The nauigations, peregrinations and voyages, made into Turkie by Nicholas Nicholay|section=I vi 4
|passage=The [[ordinance|ordinaunce]]...made such a great [[noise|noyse]] and [[thundering|thunderyng]] that it seemed '''the heaven''' would have fallen.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1594|author=Thomas Blundeville|title=M. Blundeuile his Exercises|section=act I scene 3
|passage=In ascending orderly [[upwards|vpwardes]]...The first is the [[sphere|Spheare]] of the [[Moon]]e...The [[seventh|seuenth]] the [[sphere|Spheare]] of [[Saturn]]e, The [[eighth|eight]] the [[sphere|Spheare]] of the fixed [[stars|Starres]], commonly called the firmament. The ninth is called the second [[movable|moueable]] or [[crystal|Christall]] '''heauen''', The tenth is called the first [[movable|moueable]], and the [[eleventh|eleuenth]] is called the [[empyreal|Emperiall]] '''heauen''', where God and his Angels are said to dwell.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|I|1|text=What obscured light '''the heauens''' did grant.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1625|author=Nathanæl Carpenter|title=Geography delineated forth in two bookes|volume=I|chapter=4|page=77
|passage='''The Heauens'''...are carried in 24 [[hours|houres]] from East to West.}}
##* {{RQ:Stanley History of Philosophy|volume=II|part=5|section=The Doctrine of {{w|Plato}} Delivered by [[w:Alcinous (philosopher)|Alcinous]]|chapter=XIV|chaptername=Of the Soul of the World, the Sphears and Stars|page=74|passage=God framed alſo the Stars and conſtellations; ſome ''fixed'' for the Ornament of '''Heaven''' and might, very many in number.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1930|month=March|journal=Nature|section=179 2
|passage=The moon's path lies in that belt of '''the heavens''' known as the zodiac.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1981|author=E.R. Harrison|title=Cosmology|section=XII 250
|passage=In an infinite...universe the stars would collectively outshine the Sun and flood '''the heavens''' with light far more intense than is observed.}}
##* '''2006''', Peter Carroll translating a maxim of the Southern Song dynasty in ''Between Heaven and Modernity: Reconstructing Suzhou, 1895&ndash;1937'':
##*: Above is '''Heaven''', Below are [[Suzhou]] and [[Hangzhou]]
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} The [[near]] [[sky]] in which [[weather]], [[flying]] [[animal]]s, [[etc.]] [[appear]]; {{lb|en|obsolete}} the [[atmosphere]]; the [[climate]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1581|translator=George Pettie|author=Stefano Guazzo|title=Ciuile Conuersation|section=I 26
|passage=[[every|Everie]]...[[country|Countrie]], by the nature of the place, the climate of '''the Heaven''', and the influence of the [[stars|starres]] [[hath]] [[certain]]e [[virtues|vertues]].}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merchant of Venice|IV|i|text=The [[quality|qualitie]] of [[mercy|mercie]] is not [[strained|ſtraind]],<br>it [[drops|droppeth]] as the gentle [[rain]]e from '''heauen'''<br>[[upon|vpon]] the place beneath}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1660|author=George Mackenzie|title=Religio Stoici|section=II 44
|passage=Fellow-believers...fed the birds of '''heaven''' with the [[carcasses|carcases]] of pious and reverend Church-men.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[model]] [[display]]ing the [[movement]] of the [[celestial]] [[bodies]], an [[orrery]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1600|author=Thomas Nashe|title=Summers Last Will
|passage=[[every|Euery]] man cannot, with Archimedes, make a '''heauen''' of [[brass]]e.}}
# {{lb|en|religion}} The [[abode]] of [[God]] or the [[god]]s, [[traditionally]] [[conceive]]d as beyond the [[sky]]; especially:
## {{lb|en|Christianity|usually capitalized}} The [[abode]] of [[God]] and of the [[angel]]s and [[saint]]s in [[His]] [[presence]].
##* '''1560''', {{w|Geneva Bible}}, [[w:Book of Revelation|Revelation]] 12:7&ndash;8:
##*: And there was a [[battle|battel]] in '''heauen'''. Michael & his Angels [[fought|foght]] [[against|againſt]] the dragon, and the dragon foght & his Angels. But they [[prevailed|preuailed]] not, [[neither|nether]] was their place [[found]]e [[any|anie]] more in '''heauen'''.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1644|author=w:Samuel Rutherford|title={{w|Lex, Rex}}: The Law and the Prince|section=V 16
|passage=Conſider firſt that the [[w:John Maxwell (bishop)|excommunicated Prelate]] [[saith|ſaith]]... ''Kings are not [[immediately|immediatly]] from God, as by any [[special|ſpeciall]] Ordinance ſent from '''Heaven''' by the miniſtery of Angels and Prophets, there were but ſome few ſuch, as'' Moſes, Saul, David, [[etc.]]}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|line=263|text=Better to reign in Hell, then<!--sic--> serve in '''Heav'n'''.|year=1873}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 30 1906|journal=Washington Post|section=12 4
|passage=Christ's coming from the '''heavens''' has entered into the life of humanity as the Founder of the world to come.}}
## {{lb|en|religion|by extension|often capitalized}} The [[abode]] of the [[Abrahamic]] [[God]]; [[similar]] [[abode]]s of the [[gods]] in [[other]] [[religion]]s and [[tradition]]s, [[such as]] [[mount|Mount]] [[Olympus]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus|IV|3|text=With [[Jove|Ioue]] in '''heauen''', or some where else.}}
##* '''1649''', Alexander Ross translating the Sieur Du Ryer, ''The [[w:Koran|Alcoran]] Of [[w:Muhammad|Mahomet]], Translated out of the Arabique into French... newly Englished'', 406:
##*: As he <nowiki>[</nowiki>''Muhammad''<nowiki>]</nowiki> was returning, in the fourth '''Heaven''', Moses advised him to [[go]]e back to God.
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1832|author=Charles Coleman|title=The Mythology of the Hindus|section=XIII 220
|passage=Like the Buddhas, they <nowiki>[</nowiki>''the [[Jain]]s''<nowiki>]</nowiki> believe that there is a plurality of '''heavens''' and hells.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1841|author=Mountstuart Elphinstone|title=The History of India|section=I ii iv 169
|passage='''The heaven''' of [[Siva]] is in the midst of the eternal snows and glaciers of Keilás, one of the highest and deepest groups of the stupendous summits of [[Himalaya|Hémaláya]].}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Lillian Tseng|title=Picturing Heaven in Early China|section=2
|passage=To grasp the Chinese's<!--sic--> notion of '''Heaven''', we must look at the contexts in which ''tian'' is used... In the ''Book of Odes'' (''Shi jing'' [[詩經#Chinese|詩經]]), which includes poems dated between the eleventh and seventh centuries <small>[[BCE]]</small>, ''tian'' is a place where the Heavenly [[thearch|Thearch]] resides.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension|usually capitalized}} [[providence|Providence]], the [[will]] of [[God]] or the [[council]] of the [[gods]]; [[fate]].
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|III|iv|text={{...}}he cannot [[thrive|thriue]],<br>[[unless|Vnlesse]] her prayers, whom '''heauen''' delights to [[hear]]e<br>And [[loves|loues]] to grant, [[reprieve|repreeue]] him from the wrath<br>Of greatest [[justice|Iustice]].}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Daniel|4|26|text=After that thou shalt [[have|haue]] [[known|knowen]] that '''the heauens''' [[do]]e rule.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|passage=212|text={{...}}The will<br>And high permission of all-ruling '''Heaven'''.|year=1873}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1793|author=Henry Boyd|title=Poems|section=II iv 270
|passage='''Heaven''' commands thine arm<br>To lift the sure-destroying sword!}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 8 1886|journal=The Pall Mall Gazette|section=1 1
|passage=...executing the just judgment of offended '''Heaven''' upon cattle-[[hougher]]s, traitors, and assassins.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1992|translator=W.S. Wilson|author=E. Yoshikawa|title=Taiko|section=II 186
|passage=There's nothing we can do but pray to '''heaven''' for good luck.}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Lillian Tseng|title=Picturing Heaven in Early China|section=3
|passage=Cosmologists regarded '''Heaven''' as a force&mdash;composed of ''[[qi]]'' [[氣#Chinese|氣]], which was divided into ''[[yin]]'' [[陰#Chinese|陰]] and ''[[yang]]'' [[陽#Chinese|陽]] aspects&mdash;that kept the cosmos moving.}}
# {{lb|en|religion}} The [[afterlife]] of the [[bless]]ed [[dead]], [[traditionally]] [[conceive]]d as [[oppose]]d to an [[afterlife]] of the [[wicked]] and [[unjust]] ([[compare]] {{m|en|hell}}); specifically:
#* '''1925 July 1''', {{w|Ernest Hemingway}}, letter to {{w|F. Scott Fitzgerald}}:
#*: I wonder what your idea of '''heaven''' would be&mdash;A beautiful vacuum filled with wealthy monogamists, all powerful and members of the best families drinking themselves to death. And [[hell]] would probably be an ugly vacuum full of poor polygamists unable to obtain booze... To me '''heaven''' would be a big bull ring with me holding two [[barrera]] seats and a trout stream outside that no one else was allowed to fish in and two lovely houses in the town; one where I would have my wife and children and be monogamous and love them truly and well and the other where I would have my nine beautiful mistresses on 9 different floors...
## {{lb|en|Christianity|Islam}} [[paradise|Paradise]], the [[afterlife]] of the [[soul]]s who are [[not]] [[sent]] to a [[place]] of [[punishment]] or [[purification]] [[such as]] [[hell]], [[purgatory]], or [[limbo]]; the [[state]] or [[condition]] of being in the [[presence]] of [[God]] after [[death]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1544|author=Richard Tracy|title=A supplycacion to our moste soueraigne lorde Kynge henry the eyght Kynge of England of Fraunce and of Irelande|section=C
|passage=[[teach|Teache]] the people to [[get]]t '''heuen''' with [[fasting|fastynge]].}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2|I|1|text=...what I [[speak]]e<br>My body shall make good [[upon|vpon]] this earth,<br>Or my [[divine|diuine]] [[soul]]e answer it in '''heauen'''.}}
##* {{RQ:KJV|Hebrews|4|14|text=[[we|Wee]] [[have|haue]] a great high Priest, that is passed into '''the heauens'''.}}
## {{lb|en|religion|by extension|often capitalized}} The [[afterlife]] of the [[blessed]] [[dead]] in [[other]] [[religion]]s and [[tradition]]s, [[such as]] the [[Pure Land]] or [[Elysium]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=Lillian Tseng|title=Picturing Heaven in Early China|section=3
|passage=The belief in ascending to '''Heaven''' after death became widespread in the Han dynasty.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Any [[paradise]]; any [[blissful]] [[place]] or [[experience]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|II|1|text=[[I'll|Ile]] follow thee and make a '''heauen''' of hell.}}
#* '''1660''' November 14, a speech in the House of Commons in W. Cobbett, ''Parl. Hist.'' (1808), IV 145:
#*: England, that was formerly '''the heaven''', would be now the hell for women.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|passage=254&ndash;255|text=The mind is its own place, and in it self<!--sic--><br>Can make a '''Heav'n''' of Hell, a Hell of '''Heav'n'''.|year=1873}}
#* '''1782''', F. Burney, ''Cecilia'', I iii iv 51:
#*: Such a shop as that...would be quite a '''heaven''' upon earth to me.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1940|author=w:H.G. Wells|title=w:Babes in the Darkling Wood|section=II iii 198
|passage=They thought strikes and hunger marches the quintessence of politics and Soviet Russia '''heaven''' on earth.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2002|author=Summersill Elementary School| title=Time Travel| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pKC0-ixbMCEC&pg=PA16| isbn=9780595242917| page=16| publisher=iUniverse| 1=en| passage=While eating my snack I decided to walk around the house and I saw the hallways change into beautiful valleys and oceans. The television screen appeared on the wall. It was so beautiful that I thought I was in '''heaven'''.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[state]] of [[bliss]]; a [[peaceful]] [[ecstasy]].
#* '''1550''', J. Heywood, Dialogue Prov. Eng. Tongue, II vii:
#*: [[husbands|Husbandes]] are in '''heauen'''...whose [[wives|wiues]] scold not.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 26 1809|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=The French Revolution as It Appeared to Enthusiasts at Its Commencement|journal=Friend|section=No. 11, ll. 4-5
|passage=Bliss was it in that dawn to be alive,<br>But to be young was very '''heaven'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2000|author=Veronica Brooks|title=It Could Lead to Dancing|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=6sjuG0kqXmgC&pg=PA18| isbn=9780595097968| page=18| publisher=iUniverse| 1=en| passage=He would just stare at me and say, “You are beautiful, you are so beautiful.” I was in '''heaven''' hearing this.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:DJ Sammy; w:Yanou; Frank Reinert; Bryan Adams, {{w|Jim Vallance}}; w:Bob Clearmountain| artist= w:Yanou; w:Do (singer)| title= Heaven| album= Heaven| year=2002| note= Title track| text= We're in '''heaven'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2007|author=w:Jackie Collins| title=Drop Dead Beautiful: A Novel| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bsrDCw7pp4wC&pg=PA452| isbn=9781429917209| page=452| publisher=St. Martin's Publishing Group| 1=en| passage=She was in '''heaven''' — she'd never seen so many stars gathered in one place. She already had her eye on Charlie Dollar. Oh yes, Charlie Dollar might be ancient, but he was still raging hot in a Jack Nicholson kind of way.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2008|author=w:Robert Scott| title=Driven To Murder| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gb-fBQAAQBAJ&pg=PT163| isbn=9780786038527| page=163| publisher=Pinnacle Books| 1=en| passage=“Because she was modest and beautiful and he thought he was in '''heaven''' when she was around?” “Yes, that's what he said.”}}
#* {{quote-book|year=2014|author=Joe Satriani; Jake Brown| title=Strange Beautiful Music: A Musical Memoir| pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yEQaAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA8| isbn=9781939529657| page=8| publisher=BenBella Books| 1=en| passage=I'd turn it up to 10 and it sounded all distorted and I remember feeling like I was in '''heaven'''!}}
# {{lb|en|informal|with a modifier}} [[similarly|Similarly]] [[blissful]] [[afterlives]], [[place]]s, or [[state]]s for [[particular]] [[people]], [[animal]]s, or [[object]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1867|author=J.W. De Forest|title=Miss Ravenel's Conversion|section=XXVI 368
|passage=Perhaps it has gone to '''the dog heaven''', and is wagging somewhere in glory.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1879|month=February|author=J. H. Payne|journal=Scribner's Monthly|section=470 2
|passage=His pet name for Easthampton is ‘'''Goose-heaven'''’, and he harps upon the idea eternally.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=October 5 1908|journal=Chicago Tribune|section=3 1
|passage=One gray beard who found the gates closed shinned up the fifteen foot fence...and dropped into '''the baseball heaven''' he was seeking.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1972|author=M. Sanders|title=Flash
|passage=The Dave Clark 5 deserve a place in '''Rock & Roll Heaven''' right along there beside Question Mark & The Mysterians, the Standells, Count Five, the Troggs, and the Music Machine.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 3 1986|journal=Newsweek|section=70
|passage=The building was once a candy factory, which makes it, Frazier says, '''mouse heaven'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=August 1 2003|journal=Church Times|section=28 3
|passage=Ricky bumps it into the garden, and tells me it is going to ‘'''the cooker heaven'''’. ‘Where it will be this size,’ adds his wife, her hands making the size of a brick. She means that it is off to the squasher.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=July 17 2004|journal=Western Mail|location=Cardiff|section=15
|passage=Goronwy has gone to '''goldfish heaven''' where he is swimming in a beautiful clear blue ocean with all the other fishies.}}

====Usage notes====
* Frequently capitalized as '{{m|en|Heaven}}' in all senses when regarded as a [[proper name]]. When used as a synonym for the impersonal sky, the word has typically been plural ("{{m|en|heavens}}" or "{{m|en|the heavens}}") since the 17th century, except in [[poetry]].

