===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|leaves}}

# The usually [[green]] and flat [[organ]] that represents the most [[prominent]] feature of most vegetative [[plant]]s.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2013| month=May-June| author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/william-e-conner-1 William E. Conner]| title=[http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/an-acoustic-arms-race An Acoustic Arms Race]| volume=101| issue=3| page=206–7| magazine=w:American Scientist| passage=Earless ghost swift moths become “invisible” to echolocating bats by forming mating clusters close (less than half a meter) above vegetation and effectively blending into the clutter of echoes that the bat receives from the '''leaves''' and stems around them.}}
# {{lb|en|botany}} A [[foliage leaf]] or any of the many and often considerably different [[structure]]s it can [[specialise]] into.
# Anything resembling the leaf of a plant.
# {{senseid|en|sheet of paper comprising two pages}} {{lb|en|publishing|bookbinding|advertising}} A sheet of a book, magazine, etc. (consisting of two [[page]]s, one on each face of the leaf).
#: {{hypo|en|flyleaf|looseleaf}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1900|title=Profitable Advertising|volume=10|issue=2|page=893
|passage=Heretofore advertisers have had to buy and pay for a '''leaf''' — two pages.}}
# A sheet of any substance beaten or rolled until very thin.
#: {{synonyms|en|folio|folium}}
#: {{ux|en|gold '''leaf'''}}
# One of the individual flat or curved strips of [[metal]], typically made of [[spring steel]], that make up a [[leaf spring]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931|author=w:Ion L. Idriess|title=w:Lasseter's Last Ride|location=Sydney|publisher=Angus and Robertson|page=52|passage=Lumbering down a precipitous "slideway," the Thornycroft broke two main '''leaves''' in the back spring[.]}}
# {{lb|en|in the plural}} [[tea leaf|Tea leaves]].
# A flat section used to extend the size of a table.
# {{senseid|en|panel}}{{lb|en|plural {{m|en|leaves}} or {{m|en|leafs}}}} A moveable [[panel]], e.g. of a [[bridge]] or [[door]], originally one that [[hinge]]d but now also applied to other forms of movement.
#: {{hyponyms|en|doorleaf}}
#: {{meronyms|en|stile}}
#: {{ux|en|The train car has one single-'''leaf''' and two double-'''leaf''' doors per side.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Department of Bridges, City of New York, Report|year=1914|page=90|passage=The bridge shear locks were repaired and the long ends of the shear locks shortened about two inches to eliminate butting of the bridge '''leafs''' against each other.}}
#* {{quote-us-patent|en|author=William R. Kennedy; John M. Kennedy|title=Power mine door system|url=https://patents.google.com/patent/US5222838A|patent=5,222,838|date=21 July 1992|passage=It will be noted that the pivotal mounting of the cylinders is such that the cylinders have their greatest leverage (i.e., exert the greatest door-opening force) when the door '''leafs''' 24, 28 are closed because the cylinders are generally perpendicular to the closed '''leafs'''. This is desirable because the load on the '''leafs''' is the greatest when they are closed due to air pressure. As the '''leafs''' begin to open and this air pressure decreases, the opening force exerted by the cylinders on the door '''leafs''' decreases and the opening speed of the '''leafs''' increases.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Department of Transportation and Related Agencies Appropriations for 1994: Hearings Before a Subcommittee of the Committee on Appropriations, House of Representatives, One Hundred Third Congress, First Session|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|year=1993|page=136|isbn=0-16-041074-6|passage=THE NTSB RECOMMENDS THAT THE U.S. COAST GUARD: REQUIRE OWNERS/OPERATORS OF BASCULE BRIDGES TO INSTALL CAUTION LIGHTS AND DAYLIGHT MARKINGS AT ELEVATION POINTS ON BRIDGE '''LEAFS''' WHERE THEY PROTRUDE OVER NAVIGABLE CHANNELS TO IDEN TIFY{{sic}} TO MARINERS THE POINT AT WHICH FULL SKYWARD CHANNEL CLEARANCE IS NOT AVAILABLE TO TRANSITING VESSELS: THE LIGHTS SHOULD ACTIVATE WHEN THE BRIDGE'''LEAFS''' ARE IN THE NORMAL FULLY OPEN POSITION.{{...}}THE NTSB RECOMMENDS THAT THE U.S. COAST GUARD: REQUIRE THAT BRIDGE OWNERS/OPERATORS PROVIDE IN BASCULE BRIDGE PERMIT APPLICATIONS THE ANGLE OF THE BRIDGE'''LEAF(S)'''. THE MAXIMUM VERTICAL CLEARANCE AT THE FENDERS AND AT THE BRIDGE'''LEAF''' ENDS, AND THE EXTENT OF HORIZONTAL CHANNEL CLEARANCE OVER WHICH FULL SKYWARD CLEARANCE IS AVAILABLE WHEN THE BRIDGE '''LEAFS''' ARE IN THE FULLY OPEN POSITION.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=Realty and Building|year=1993|page=20|passage=The four separate segments of the movable bridge span, known as bridge '''leafs''', were alternately rehabilitated while maintaining upper-level roadway and pedestrian traffic on one-half of the bridge and waterway traffic on one-half of the river channel.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Americana Annual: An Encyclopedia of Current Events|publisher=w:Grolier|year=1993|page=224|isbn=0-7172-0224-0|issn=0196-0180|lccn=23-10041|passage=The hydraulic system lifts and rotates two 413-ft (126-m) bridge '''leafs''', each weighing 7,500 tons, in just four minutes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gerard R. Wolfe|title=Chicago in and Around the Loop: Walking Tours of Architecture and History|publisher=[[w:S&P Global|McGraw-Hill]]|year=1996|page=150|isbn=0-07-071390-1|passage=Note the Art Deco-style sculptures on the wall of the bridge house showing a ship passing under the raised bridge '''leafs'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Newport-Chiakulas|title=Bridges to Justice: Love, Murder, & Politics in Chicago|publisher=w:iUniverse|year=2010|page=55|isbn=978-0-595-46683-2|passage=Like I’ve said, John, you’ve got to get the two bridge '''leafs''' to come down very slowly to a complete stop in order to trigger the tongue locks. This is what holds the two '''leafs''' of the bridge firmly in place. Otherwise, the bridge '''leafs''' will start bouncing, and once they start bouncing, you’ll never stop them from goin’ straight up in the air.}}
# {{lb|en|computing|mathematics}} In a [[tree]], a [[node]] that has no [[descendant]]s.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2011|author=John Mongan; Noah Kindler; Eric Giguère|title=Programming Interviews Exposed
|passage=The algorithm pops the stack to obtain a new current node when there are no more children (when it reaches a '''leaf''').}}
# The [[layer]] of [[fat]] supporting the [[kidney]]s of a [[pig]], [[leaf fat]].
# One of the [[tooth|teeth]] of a [[pinion]], especially when small.
# {{lb|en|slang|uncountable}} [[cannabis|Cannabis]].
# {{lb|en|4chan|internet slang|humorous|sometimes pejorative|plural '''{{m|en|leafs}}'''}} A [[Canadian]] person.
# {{lb|en|programming|[[x86]]}} A particular value of the EAX register when a [[program#Noun|program]] runs the {{w|CPUID}} instruction; each leaf represents a different category of information returned about the [[processor#Noun|processor]].
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Perry Yuan|title=[PATCH 00/11] AMD Pstate Driver Fixes and Improvements|site=[[w:Linux kernel mailing list|Kernel Lore]]|url=https://lore.kernel.org/all/CYYPR12MB86556FDDBC0843970569177B9CE62@CYYPR12MB8655.namprd12.prod.outlook.com/T/|date=7 May 2024|passage=X86_FEATURE_HETERO_CORE_TOPOLOGY is used to identify whether the processor support heterogeneous core type by reading CPUID '''leaf''' Fn_0x80000026_EAX and bit 30. if the bit is set as one, then amd_pstate driver will check EBX 30:28 bits to get the core type.}}

